  at our site we've had one or two segmentation faults ruin a game but the
more common problem for us was system crashes (not related to the game)! we
finally implemented a "failsafe" shell-script which runs the game. the script
makes a backup copy of a saved game when the game is replayed. in the event
of natural disasters the backup is restored as the saved game. all of this
takes place invisibly to the user (except for the prompt to remove a game
in progress) unless the user checks his disk usage! a fully explored dungeon
(60 levels) can take up to ~750k on a unix system and with a backup that's
1.5M per game!! since nethack3.0 will invariably be bigger (and better?) and
if it implements a "failsafe" scheme, the disk usage requirement would be
around 2.5M!!! what was that??? ... i think i'm hearing new sounds in the
dungeon ...

  a snapshot feature would be nice so that you wouldn't have to interrupt
the game in order to get your progress on disk for safe keeping. will
nethack3.0 require hard drives for its personal computer users?

